          "type": "turn",
          "question": "At what blood pressure reading is hypertension diagnosed, and do the major bodies agree?",
          "answer": "No emergency is indicated by this question. The material below is general information, not a diagnosis for you or anyone else.\n\n**The thresholds differ by guidance.** The sources I searched do not agree on one cut-off.\n\n- **US cardiology guidance** (Major cardiology society consensus guidance, \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\") sorts adult office readings like this:\n  - Normal: top number under 120 and bottom number under 80.\n  - Elevated: top number 120 to 129, bottom number under 80.\n  - Stage 1 hypertension: top number 130 to 139, or bottom number 80 to 89.\n  - Stage 2 hypertension: top number at least 140, or bottom number at least 90.\n  - When the two numbers fall in different categories, the higher one applies.\n- **European guidance**, according to the same document, describes office hypertension as starting at 140/90. It treats 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 as \"high-normal\" rather than stage 1.\n\n**Why one reading is not enough.** The general clinical reference (\"Hypertension (high blood pressure)\") explains that pressure changes through the day, so a diagnosis rests on repeated measurements. A single high reading in a clinic is not itself a diagnosis. The condition often has no symptoms and is usually found on routine checks.\n\n**What the sources did not cover.** The passages did not say which convention a particular clinic or country uses, and they did not give guidance for children or pregnancy.\n\nIf you have a reading you are worried about, the sensible next step is to raise it with a clinician, who can repeat the measurement and tell you which guidance applies to you.\n\n*Sources: \"Hypertension (high blood pressure)\" (general clinical reference); \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\" (Major cardiology society consensus guidance).*\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
